tree whoop_android_interview "WHOOP Android Engineer Interview Simulation"
@view tree

topic opening "Opening and first impression"
  @text "Start with a short story: current Android experience, health relevance, and the reason this role fits."
  @feature "Keep the opening under 90 seconds"
  @priority high
  question tell_about_yourself "Can you tell me a little about yourself and your background in Android development?"
    @feature "Opening question"
    response ideal_introduction "Computer Engineering graduate and Android developer"
      @answer "Hi, nice to meet you too. I am a Computer Engineering graduate from Turkey. I worked as an Android Developer at RealWorks for more than one year on a health application using Kotlin, MVVM, Room, Coroutines, WorkManager and Health Connect. Before that, I worked with Kotlin, XML and REST APIs during my internship at Enerjisa. I also built Neuralith AI Studio with Kotlin Multiplatform and Compose Multiplatform. I enjoy Android development, especially building real products and solving technical problems."
      @feature "Present → relevant evidence → role fit"
      @tag preferred opening
      @priority high
      followup ownership_opening "What did you own in your most recent project?"
        @text "Describe personal responsibility, not only what the team delivered."
        @feature "Probe: ownership and scope"
        response ownership_answer "Personal ownership and technical decisions"
          @answer "I owned the parts of the feature from implementation through integration. I worked on the API contract, client state, background behavior and the checks needed to make the rollout safe. When there was a trade-off, I documented it, discussed it in review and used production signals to validate the decision."
          @feature "Responsibility → decision → evidence"
    response concise_introduction "Short version"
      @answer "I am a Computer Engineering graduate and Android developer from Turkey. I have professional experience with Kotlin, MVVM, Room, Coroutines, WorkManager and Health Connect in a health application. I also built a Kotlin Multiplatform project, Neuralith AI Studio. I enjoy solving real Android and product problems."
      @feature "Use when the interviewer wants a quick answer"

topic motivation "Motivation and Android direction"
  @text "Connect health data, wearable technology, Android engineering, and your own relevant experience."
  question why_whoop "Why are you interested in WHOOP?"
    response whoop_answer "Health, Android, and wearable technology"
      @answer "I am interested in WHOOP because it combines Android development, health data and wearable technology. In my previous job, I worked with health features and Google Health Connect, so this area is already meaningful to me. I also read WHOOP engineering articles and liked the real technical challenges around background work, performance and device communication. I would like to learn from experienced Android engineers while contributing to a product with real user impact."
      @feature "Health experience → technical interest → learning goal"
      @tag preferred motivation
      followup whoop_engineering "What did you read about WHOOP engineering?"
        response engineering_article "Background work and wake locks"
          @answer "I read about an Android performance problem related to background work and wake locks. I liked that the engineering team explained the real problem, how they investigated it and how they improved the application. It showed me that WHOOP takes Android performance seriously."
          @feature "Mention one concrete technical topic"
  question why_android "Why did you choose Android development?"
    response android_reason "Building products people use directly"
      @answer "I like Android because I can build something that people use directly. Android development also includes more than UI. You need to think about lifecycle, networking, background work, performance and different devices. I enjoy solving these practical problems and seeing the result in a real product."
      @feature "Product impact and systems thinking"
  question why_hire "Why should we hire you?"
    response hire_answer "Early-career engineer with real product experience"
      @answer "I already have real Android development experience, but I am still early in my career and very open to learning. I have worked with health features, Kotlin, Coroutines, APIs and Android background work. I also built my own product, so I am comfortable learning new technologies and solving problems independently. I think I can contribute while learning a lot from the WHOOP engineering team."
      @feature "Experience + humility + learning speed"
      @tag preferred

topic professional_experience "Professional Android experience"
  @text "Use the RealWorks story first, then expand into background execution and health data when asked."
  question realworks "Can you tell me more about your Android experience at RealWorks?"
    response realworks_answer "HaloScape health application"
      @answer "At RealWorks, I worked on a health application called HaloScape. I helped improve the application architecture using MVVM, Room and Coroutines. I also worked on health features such as fasting tracking and metabolic age. One important part was Google Health Connect integration for health and wearable data. I used WorkManager and Foreground Services for background health-related tasks."
      @feature "Health features and Android architecture"
      @tag preferred realworks
      followup workmanager_use "What did you use WorkManager for?"
        response workmanager_answer "Reliable deferred background work"
          @answer "I used WorkManager for background tasks that should continue reliably even if the application is not open. For example, health-related synchronization or background processing should not depend only on the Activity lifecycle. WorkManager is useful when the work can be scheduled and retried under system constraints."
          @feature "Reliability, constraints, and retry behavior"
      followup worker_service_difference "What is the difference between WorkManager and a Foreground Service?"
        response worker_service_answer "Deferred work versus visible long-running work"
          @answer "WorkManager is good for reliable background tasks that do not need to run immediately all the time. A Foreground Service is better when the user needs to know that an important long-running task is active. A Foreground Service also shows a notification and should be used only when the work is user-visible and justified."
          @feature "Explain the user-visible trade-off"

topic android_fundamentals "Android fundamentals"
  question kotlin "How comfortable are you with Kotlin?"
    response kotlin_answer "Kotlin as the main Android language"
      @answer "Kotlin is my main language for Android development. I have used classes, data classes, nullable types, collections, lambdas and Coroutines. I also use Kotlin in my Kotlin Multiplatform project. I am comfortable with Kotlin fundamentals, while I am still learning more advanced parts of the language."
      @feature "Confident fundamentals with honest scope"
      followup kotlin_like "What do you like about Kotlin?"
        response kotlin_like_answer "Null safety and readable asynchronous code"
          @answer "I like null safety because it helps prevent many common errors. I also like Coroutines because asynchronous code is easier to read and maintain compared to deeply nested callbacks."
          @feature "Null safety and structured concurrency"
  question coroutines "Can you give me an example of where you used Coroutines?"
    response coroutine_answer "Streaming responses with Kotlin Flow"
      @answer "In Neuralith AI Studio, I use Coroutines for API requests and AI response streaming. The client receives response parts from the server and exposes them as a Kotlin Flow. The UI collects the Flow and updates the message while the response is coming. I also move CPU-heavy work, such as Base64 conversion, away from the UI thread."
      @feature "Flow, streaming, and dispatcher choice"
      @tag preferred technical
      followup coroutine_importance "Why is this important?"
        response coroutine_importance_answer "Keep the main thread responsive"
          @answer "Heavy work on the main thread can make the application slow or freeze the UI. Coroutines help me move work to the correct dispatcher and keep the UI responsive. They also make cancellation and lifecycle-aware work easier to reason about."
          @feature "Responsiveness and cancellation"
  question rest_api "Have you worked with REST APIs?"
    response rest_answer "Ktor, JSON models, and authenticated requests"
      @answer "Yes. I first worked with REST APIs during my internship at Enerjisa. In Neuralith, I use Ktor for API communication. The application sends authenticated requests to my Go backend, and I use Kotlin serializable data classes for request and response models. I have worked with GET, POST and other HTTP operations, JSON data and bearer token authentication."
      @feature "Client contract and authentication"
      followup api_errors "How do you handle API errors?"
        response api_errors_answer "Separate service errors from network failures"
          @answer "It depends on the error. For example, Neuralith handles insufficient credit differently from a normal network error. For temporary network problems, I try not to remove the user session immediately. I also use different timeout values for different operations and show the user an actionable state when recovery is possible."
          @feature "Error category → user experience → recovery"
  question mvvm "Do you have experience with MVVM?"
    response mvvm_answer "Professional MVVM experience with a nuanced project explanation"
      @answer "Yes. I used MVVM in my professional Android work at RealWorks. In Neuralith, the current architecture is different and most screen state is still kept directly in Compose. If I refactor Neuralith, I would move screen state and business logic into ViewModels, expose UI state with StateFlow and move API access behind repositories."
      @feature "State the experience honestly"
      @tag preferred architecture
      followup viewmodel_reason "Why use a ViewModel?"
        response viewmodel_answer "Separate UI state from application logic"
          @answer "A ViewModel helps separate UI code from application logic. It also survives configuration changes better than state stored only inside an Activity or Composable. This separation makes the code easier to test and gives the screen a clear state owner."
          @feature "Lifecycle, separation, and testability"

topic project_and_debugging "Project and debugging stories"
  question neuralith_project "Tell me about your Neuralith AI Studio project."
    response neuralith_answer "Kotlin Multiplatform AI client and Go backend"
      @answer "Neuralith AI Studio is a project I built to provide access to different AI providers from one application. The client uses Kotlin Multiplatform and Compose Multiplatform for Android and iOS. I share UI, networking and models between platforms. The backend is written in Go and uses PostgreSQL and Redis. One feature I enjoyed building was AI response streaming using Server-Sent Events and Kotlin Flow."
      @feature "Describe the product without overclaiming"
      @tag project technical
      note architecture_wording "Say modular Go backend service, not a complete Go microservices architecture."
        @feature "Keep the claim precise"
  question technical_problem "Tell me about a technical problem you solved."
    response mediaplayer_story "MediaPlayer lifecycle crash in Compose"
      @answer "One Android problem happened with a native MediaPlayer inside my Compose application. After the application went to the background, the MediaPlayer reference could become invalid. If the user tried to seek the video, the application could crash. I fixed it by clearing the player reference when AndroidView was released and checking the player state before using seek. This taught me that native Android components still have their own lifecycle even when I use Compose."
      @feature "Problem → lifecycle cause → safe fix → lesson"
      @tag preferred debugging lifecycle
      @priority high
  question multiplatform "I saw Kotlin Multiplatform on your resume. How did you use it?"
    response multiplatform_answer "Share common code and keep platform boundaries clear"
      @answer "I use Kotlin Multiplatform to share most of the application code between Android and iOS. For example, I share Compose UI, API models, networking and application logic. For platform-specific features, I use expect and actual implementations. Video playback, file downloading and authentication can have different Android and iOS implementations."
      @feature "Shared code with explicit native boundaries"
      followup not_everything "Why not share everything?"
        response not_everything_answer "Native APIs have platform-specific behavior"
          @answer "Some features depend on native platform APIs. Android and iOS have different SDKs for some system features, so I prefer to share common logic while keeping native integrations platform-specific. This keeps the shared layer useful without hiding important platform differences."
          @feature "Share intent, preserve native behavior"

topic technical_depth "Technical depth and self-awareness"
  question ai_workflow "WHOOP uses AI tools a lot internally. How do you use AI in your development workflow?"
    response ai_workflow_answer "AI as an assistant with human ownership"
      @answer "I use AI as a development assistant, but I do not trust the output automatically. I use it for debugging, understanding code, creating a first implementation and comparing different solutions. After that, I review the code, understand what it does and test the important paths. AI helps me work faster, but I am still responsible for the final code."
      @feature "Use AI → review assumptions → test important paths"
      @tag preferred ai
      followup ai_correctness "How do you know AI-generated code is correct?"
        response ai_correctness_answer "Understand, review, and test"
          @answer "First, I make sure I understand the code. Then I review the changes and think about success and error cases. For important logic, I run tests. If the AI makes an assumption that is not correct for my project, I change or reject the solution."
          @feature "Understanding is part of verification"
  question weakness "What is one area of Android development you want to improve?"
    response testing_weakness "Automated Android testing"
      @answer "One area I want to improve is automated Android testing. I have more experience building features and solving production problems than writing a large Android test suite. I understand the importance of unit tests and UI tests, and I want to get stronger with ViewModel testing, Coroutine testing and Compose UI testing."
      @feature "Real gap + concrete learning direction"
      @tag preferred learning
  question senior_feedback "How do you feel about working under more experienced engineers and receiving feedback?"
    response senior_feedback_answer "Feedback as an engineering advantage"
      @answer "I see it as a big advantage. I like building things independently, but I know I still have a lot to learn. If a senior engineer gives me feedback, I want to understand why and improve my solution. I do not think my first solution is always the best solution. Code review and feedback are part of becoming a better engineer."
      @feature "Curiosity, humility, and action"
  question fast_environment "WHOOP is a fast-paced company. How do you feel about working in that kind of environment?"
    response fast_environment_answer "Move quickly while protecting reliability"
      @answer "I am comfortable with a fast environment if priorities are clear. In my own project, I often had to learn new technologies and solve problems quickly. But speed should not mean ignoring code quality. I try to find a balance between delivering the feature and keeping the code reliable."
      @feature "Speed with clear priorities and quality"

topic closing "Relocation and closing"
  @text "Finish with an honest relocation answer and one or two thoughtful questions for the interviewer."
  question relocation "This role is based in Boston. Are you prepared to relocate?"
    response relocation_yes "Relocation answer if true"
      @answer "Yes. I understand that this is an on-site position in Boston, and I am prepared to relocate if I receive the opportunity."
      @feature "Adjust this answer to your real situation"
      @tag adapt honestly
      note sponsorship "If asked about immigration sponsorship, answer honestly according to your legal situation."
        @feature "Never guess or hide the requirement"
  question interviewer_questions "Do you have any questions for me?"
    response success_question "Ask about first-six-month success"
      @answer "What does success look like for an entry-level Android engineer during the first six months at WHOOP?"
      @feature "Best general closing question"
      @tag preferred closing
    response mentorship_question "Ask about support and mentorship"
      @answer "How does the Android team usually support junior engineers through code reviews and mentorship?"
      @feature "Shows learning orientation"
    response technical_closing_question "Ask about the first project"
      @answer "What kind of Android projects would someone in this role work on first?"
      @feature "Good technical alternative"
  question final_fit "Why are you a good fit for this role?"
    response final_close "Close with confidence and learning mindset"
      @answer "I bring real Android experience from a health product, hands-on work with Kotlin and background execution, and the curiosity to keep improving. I am early in my career, but I am comfortable owning problems, asking clear questions and learning from feedback. WHOOP is a strong fit because the product connects the Android and health areas I want to develop in."
      @feature "Relevant experience → working style → fit"

topic rescue_phrases "Useful phrases during the interview"
  note repeat_question "Sorry, could you repeat the question, please?"
    @text "Use this when you did not understand the question."
  note slower_question "Could you say that a little more slowly, please?"
    @text "Use this when you need more processing time."
  note clarify_term "Could you explain what you mean by that?"
    @text "Use this when a technical term or question is unclear."
  note thinking_time "That's a good question. Let me think for a second."
    @text "A natural way to gain a moment before answering."
  note limited_experience "I haven't used that directly yet, but I am familiar with the concept."
    @text "Use this instead of inventing experience."
  note reframe_answer "Sorry, let me explain that in a better way."
    @text "Use this when you want to correct or sharpen an answer."

topic story_bank "Five technical stories to remember"
  @text "Choose the story that matches the question instead of trying to tell every story at once."
  example health_connect_story "RealWorks — Health Connect"
    @text "Use for health data, wearable relevance, permissions, and Android integrations."
    @feature "WHOOP relevance"
  example background_work_story "RealWorks — WorkManager and Foreground Service"
    @text "Use for lifecycle, scheduling, constraints, notifications, and reliable background execution."
    @feature "Android fundamentals"
  example mediaplayer_story_bank "Neuralith — MediaPlayer lifecycle crash"
    @text "Use for Compose, AndroidView, native lifecycle, debugging, and defensive state checks."
    @feature "Strong production-style debugging story"
  example streaming_story "Neuralith — AI response streaming"
    @text "Use for Coroutines, Kotlin Flow, Server-Sent Events, dispatchers, and responsive UI."
    @feature "Concurrency and networking"
  example multiplatform_story "Neuralith — Kotlin Multiplatform"
    @text "Use for shared code, expect and actual, native boundaries, and Android-iOS trade-offs."
    @feature "Cross-platform architecture"
